    code: { lang: "java", title: "ExceptionsDemo.java — complete, runnable (Java 17)", code: "class InvalidPortException extends Exception {\n    InvalidPortException(String msg) { super(msg); }\n}\n\npublic class ExceptionsDemo {\n    static int parsePort(String text) throws InvalidPortException {\n        int port;\n        try {\n            port = Integer.parseInt(text);\n        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {\n            throw new InvalidPortException(\"not a number: \" + text);\n        }\n        if (port < 1 || port > 65535) {\n            throw new InvalidPortException(\"out of range: \" + port);\n        }\n        return port;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String[] inputs = {\"443\", \"ssh\", \"70000\"};\n        for (String in : inputs) {\n            try {\n                System.out.println(in + \" -> accepted \" + parsePort(in));\n            } catch (InvalidPortException e) {\n                System.out.println(in + \" -> rejected: \" + e.getMessage());\n            } finally {\n                System.out.println(\"  (attempt logged)\");\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}", output: "443 -> accepted 443\n  (attempt logged)\nssh -> rejected: not a number: ssh\n  (attempt logged)\n70000 -> rejected: out of range: 70000\n  (attempt logged)", lines: ["parsePort converts a low-level NumberFormatException into the DOMAIN exception — callers see one meaningful error type.", "The range failure (70000) raises the same custom type: two causes, one contract.", "finally prints after EVERY iteration — success and rejection alike — the always-runs guarantee.", "main does not declare throws: it HANDLES the exception, which is the other legal option the compiler offers."] },
    mistakes: ["catch (Exception e) { } — swallowing everything, including your own bugs (the bare-except anti-pattern).", "Using exceptions for normal control flow (expected 'not found' as an exception storm) — they are for failures, not answers.", "Declaring throws Exception on everything — a contract that says nothing."],
    security: "Unhandled exceptions leak stack traces (information disclosure) and crash services (availability). Specific catches, domain exceptions, and finally-cleanup are the Java form of controlled failure — the same principle as Python's exceptions lesson, compiler-enforced.",
    pc: ["Run ExceptionsDemo.java; add input \"0\" and predict its line before running.", "Move the logging OUT of finally into the try only — observe which lines vanish and explain."],
    android: ["Android: package names (com.example.app) are the app's identity — this lesson's package concept is on every manifest."],
    lab: "Guided lab: in the Playground, write parseAttempts(String) with a custom exception for non-numbers and negatives; test '5', 'x', '-2'.",
    mission: "Mission: explain checked exceptions to a Python programmer in two sentences (what does the compiler force?).",
    practical: "Practical assessment: trace try/catch/finally execution and choose the right exception design.",
    quiz: [
      { q: "throws InvalidPortException on parsePort means:", options: ["The method always throws", "Callers must catch it or declare it themselves — failure is part of the contract, compiler-enforced", "It is optional documentation", "The method is broken"], answer: 1, why: "Checked exceptions make handling unavoidable — ignore is not a compilable option." },
      { q: "finally's guarantee:", options: ["Runs only on success", "Runs ALWAYS — after try, after catch, even on return — cleanup's home", "Runs first", "Runs once per program"], answer: 1, why: "The always-block: resources close and attempts log whatever happened." },
      { q: "catch (Exception e) { /* empty */ } is:", options: ["Robust", "The swallowing anti-pattern — bugs and failures vanish silently", "Required by Java", "Fast"], answer: 1, why: "Catch the specific type you can handle; let the rest surface loudly in development." },
    ],
