    code: { lang: "java", title: "CollectionsDemo.java — complete, runnable (Java 17)", code: "import java.util.*;\n\npublic class CollectionsDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List<String> queue = new ArrayList<>();\n        queue.add(\"lab-web\");\n        queue.add(\"lab-old\");\n        queue.add(\"lab-web\");                       // duplicates allowed in a List\n        System.out.println(\"list size=\" + queue.size() + \" first=\" + queue.get(0));\n\n        Set<String> unique = new HashSet<>(queue);  // Set removes duplicates\n        System.out.println(\"unique=\" + unique.size());\n\n        Map<String, Integer> counts = new HashMap<>();\n        for (String h : queue) {\n            counts.merge(h, 1, Integer::sum);       // the counting idiom\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"lab-web count=\" + counts.get(\"lab-web\"));\n        System.out.println(\"has lab-db? \" + counts.containsKey(\"lab-db\"));\n\n        List<String> sorted = new ArrayList<>(unique);\n        Collections.sort(sorted);\n        System.out.println(\"sorted unique=\" + sorted);\n    }\n}", output: "list size=3 first=lab-web\nunique=2\nlab-web count=2\nhas lab-db? false\nsorted unique=[lab-old, lab-web]", lines: ["The List kept all 3 entries including the duplicate — order and duplicates are its contract.", "HashSet(queue) deduplicates by construction: 3 in, 2 stored.", "merge(h, 1, Integer::sum): first sighting stores 1, repeats add — Python's .get(k, 0) + 1 in one call.", "The sorted copy is a LIST made from the set: sets promise no order, so reports sort explicitly."] },
