    code: { lang: "java", title: "FilesDemo.java — complete, runnable (Java 17)", code: "import java.io.*;\nimport java.nio.file.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class FilesDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        Path file = Path.of(\"scan.txt\");\n        Files.write(file, List.of(\"host=lab-web port=443\", \"host=lab-old port=23\"));\n\n        List<String> lines = Files.readAllLines(file);\n        System.out.println(\"lines=\" + lines.size());\n        for (String line : lines) {\n            System.out.println(\"read: \" + line);\n        }\n\n        try (BufferedReader r = Files.newBufferedReader(file)) {\n            System.out.println(\"first via reader: \" + r.readLine());\n        }   // r is closed HERE automatically, on every path\n    }\n}", output: "lines=2\nread: host=lab-web port=443\nread: host=lab-old port=23\nfirst via reader: host=lab-web port=443", lines: ["main declares throws IOException — the checked exception is handled by declaration here; production code would catch and report.", "The program writes its own input first (self-contained, like the C files lesson) — scan.txt appears in the working directory.", "readAllLines returns BOTH lines as a List — fine at this size, a memory bug at 4 GB; the reader below is the scalable form.", "try-with-resources: no explicit close() call exists in the code, and the reader is still guaranteed closed."] },
